+## 1. The address of a vector element — 10 tests, the widest single cause
+
+Ten refusals whose sentences look unrelated come from one emitter gap: `&v[0]`, `&v.front()`,
+`&v[i]` and `&v[i]+n` are lowered to something that is neither an iterator nor a pointer, and
+each consumer complains in its own words.
+
+Reproduced, three snippets, all refusals:
+
+```c++
+std::vector<int> v={3,1,4,2,5}; std::sort(&v[0], &v[0]+5);            // "an iterator this translator cannot follow back to its container"
+std::copy(v.begin(), &v[3], std::ostream_iterator<double>(cout, " ")); // same refusal, other end
+Long_t i = TMath::BinarySearch(5, &k[0], e);                          // ValueError: setting an array element with a sequence
+```
+
+What ROOT does: `&v[0]` is a `T*` into the vector's own buffer, so `std::sort(&v[0], &v[0]+n)`
+sorts the vector **in place**, `std::copy` reads its elements, and a `T*` argument to
+`TMath::BinarySearch` is the array. The fix has to pass a view (container + offset + length),
+not a copy, or the in-place writes disappear.
+
+| test | line | refusal |
+|---|---|---|
+| `mathcore-testTMath` | 150 | `sort(&sa[0], &sa[0]+n)` |
+| `mathcore-testSortOrder` | 67 | `std::sort(&indexS[0], &indexS[0]+n, CompareDesc<const T*>(&k[0]))` |
+| `mathcore-stdsort` | 78 | the same, timed |
+| `mathcore-SparseDataComparer` | 97 | `std::copy(x1, &x1[ndim], …)` — "a range whose two ends are in different containers" |
+| `mathcore-SparseFit3` | 120 | the same |
+| `mathcore-SparseFit4` | 130 | the same |
+| `mathcore-binarySearchTime` | 48 | `TMath::BinarySearch(n, &k[0], elem)` |
+| `mathcore-testSpecFuncBeta` | 28 (via `&x[0]` at 84) | `TGraph(n, x, y)` given `&x[0]`, `&yb[0]` |
+| `mathmore-testGSLIntegration` | 35 | `Polynomial::SetParameters(&p.front())` |
+| `mathcore-testGraphFit` | 72 | `TGraphErrors(n, &x.front(), &y.front(), 0, &e.front())` — *"xerr has 500 bars for 1 points"*, because a `&v.front()` argument arrives as one value |
+
+`stressTMath` (bucket 4) hits the same pointer range one line after `std::generate` exists.
+
+## 2. `ROOT::Math::Random<T>` — 4 tests
+
+`Random<MersenneTwisterEngine>`, `Random<MixMaxEngine240>`, `Random<GSLRngMT>`,
+`Random<GSLRngRanLux>`, … and `RandomMT`, `QuasiRandomSobol`, `QuasiRandomNiederreiter`.
+ROOT's `Random<E>` is one header over an engine kind, with `Uniform`, `Gaus`, `Poisson`,
+`Exp`, `BreitWigner`, `Landau` and the rest — the same distributions `randoms.py` already
+implements over `TRandom`.
+
+First step is not the distribution maths (there is none new) but the **engines**: xrdroot's
+`random/mersenne.py`, `tausworthe.py` and `ranlux.py` are `TRandom3`, `TRandom2` and
+`TRandom1` — the streams exist but not under an engine name. MixMax and the eight GSL
+`GSLRng*` engines do not exist at all, and `testRandomDist` compares every distribution
+against ROOT's own numbers, so an engine that is merely "a" generator fails on the first
+value. `testMathRandom` only compares engines against each other, which is why it is the
+one to make pass first.
+
+## 3. MathMore's GSL layer — 4 tests
+
+* `assoc_laguerre` (`testSpecFunc`) — a GSL special function name reached as `ROOT.assoc_laguerre`;
+  `scipy.special.eval_genlaguerre` is the same function. **Cheapest single refusal in this
+  bucket**, and `testSpecFunc` walks every special function in turn, so the other names it
+  uses (already present?) are worth checking in the same pass.
+* `WrappedFunction<PdfFunction>` (`testStatFunc`) — `IGenFunction` over any callable; an
+  adapter, like `WrappedTF1` at `rmath.py:534`.
+* `MultiRootFinder` (`testMultiRootFinder`) — runs several `GSLRootFinder`s and keeps the
+  best; `mathtools.py:165` `RootFinder` and `numerics/rootfinder.py` are the pieces.
+* `GSLSimAnFunc` (`simanTSP`) — GSL's simulated annealing, subclassed by the test
+  (`class MySimAnFunc : public GSLSimAnFunc`), so it needs a real virtual interface, and
+  GSL's annealing schedule to match `.ref` numbers.
+
+## 4. Three `std` algorithms the emitter does not know — 3 tests
+
+`std::generate` (`stressTMath:37`), `std::shuffle` with `std::default_random_engine{}`
+(`testSampleQuantiles:42`), `std::next_permutation` (`testPermute:67`). The refusal
+("ROOT has std.generate") is not a missing name in a Python `std` namespace — algorithms
+are emitted by `pycxx` — it is the emitter not recognising the call, so it falls through to
+an attribute lookup. `stressTMath` needs bucket 1's pointer range as well.
+
+## 5. The k-d tree family — 3 tests
+
+`testkdTreeBinning` (`TKDTreeBinning(DATASZ, DATADIM, smp, NBINS)`), `newKDTreeTest`
+(`ROOT::Math::KDTree<TDataPoint<DIM>>`, built over a `std::vector<const DP*>` — the
+`std` stand-in refuses the pointer-to-template type), and `kDTreeTest`, whose **first line
+in the way** is
+
+```c++
+ProcInfo_t procInfo;  gSystem->GetProcInfo(&procInfo);  return procInfo.fMemVirtual;
+```
+
+`ProcInfo_t` is five fields (`fCpuUser`, `fCpuSys`, `fMemResident`, `fMemVirtual`, …) and
+`TSystem::GetProcInfo` is a `getrusage`; that unblocks the test only up to its tree.
+`tests/refmachine.py`-style exactness is not at stake (those are machine numbers), so a
+`resource.getrusage` fill is honest.
+
+## 6/7. Minuit2 — 2 refusals, 1 timeout
+
+`ROOT::Minuit2::Minuit2Minimizer` is the only name missing from an otherwise complete
+`Minuit2` namespace (`pyroot/minuit2.py`), and `ROOT::Math::Minimizer` already exists in
+`mathtools.py:467` with `SetMaxFunctionCalls`, `SetTolerance`, `Minimize`, `Minos`,
+`SetVariable`, … So `Minuit2Minimizer` is a subclass whose constructor maps
+`kMigrad/kSimplex/kCombined/kScan` onto the algorithm names `Minimizer` already takes.
+Two tests (`demoMinimizer`, `testADMinim`) stop on the constructor; both then run the
+Rosenbrock/AD minimisations through the core, which is iminuit-backed.
+
+## 8–12. `ROOT::Math` names with no class behind them — 5 tests
+
+In cost order: `Derivator` (the Richardson helpers in `function/numeric.py` are ROOT's own
+algorithm), `ChebyshevApprox`, `WrappedParamFunction` (2 tests for ~15 lines — the best
+ratio here), `Interpolator` (GSL kinds), `GoFTest` (must match ROOT's own approximations).
+
+`IParametricFunctionMultiDimTempl<T>` is in this list only because that is where the name is
+looked up; it is not a missing name, see below.
+
+## 13–18. Plain missing classes and methods — 6 tests
+
+`TStatistic`, `Fitter::LikelihoodFit`, `RootFinder::Name`, `MnUserCovariance::operator()=`,
+`TMatrixT::Randomize`, `std::cout.width()`. Each is small and each is in a file this worker
+does not own (`stats.py`, `mathtools.py`, `minuit2.py`, `linalg/matrices.py`,
+`cppstd/streams.py`).
+
+`std::cout.width()` is a one-attribute bug with a clear shape: `cppstd/streams.py:123` sets
+`self.width = 0` as state while `precision()` (`:193`) is a method, so `cout.width(12)` calls
+an `int`. Making `width` a method like `precision` — and keeping the state under another
+name — is the whole fix.
+
+## 19–22. Translator one-offs — 4 tests
+
+`std::max_element(...)->name`, the `(*a[N])[M]` declarator, `WrappedMemFunction`'s
+pointer-to-member template argument, and the runaway `testBinarySearch` instantiation.
+Each blocks exactly one test and each is in `pycxx`/`pycint`; the `max_element` one is the
+smallest (the `*std::max_element` emission and the `EXTREMES` table are already there).
+
+## What I found but did not fix
+
+I added **no** name to `rmath.py`. The two candidates I checked honestly are not one-liners:
+
+* `IParametricFunctionMultiDimTempl<T>` would have to be an alias of `IParamMultiFunction`,
+  but the test declares it as a *template base* —
+  `template<class T> class Func : public ROOT::Math::IParametricFunctionMultiDimTempl<T>`
+  (`testLogLExecPolicy.cxx:23-24`) — so an alias would only move the refusal from the name
+  lookup to the instantiation, and I would be claiming a fix I have not measured. It also
+  then needs `ROOT::Fit::Fitter` to run the same fit under two execution policies.
+* `RootFinder::Name()` is two names (`Name` and `Iterations`) over a seven-way kind table,
+  and lives in `mathtools.py:165`, not `rmath.py`.
+
+Also worth queueing from the reading:
+
+* `mathcore-testSpecFuncBetaI` **passes** while `mathcore-testSpecFuncBeta` fails — the
+  difference is only the `drawPoints(&x[0], &yb[0], …)` call, so bucket 1 probably turns a
+  refusal into a PASS-and-a-DIFF-check in one step. Worth rerunning those two together.
+* `stressTF1` ran **27.4 s** before its refusal at `cout.width(12)`. Fixing the stream
+  formatting opens the rest of a 27-second test on a loaded machine — check its runtime
+  against the 300 s timeout before counting it a win.
+* `testBinnedFitExecPolicy` needs `std::max_element(...)->name` and then runs a binned fit
+  under several execution policies; `Minimizer` has no `SetExecutionPolicy` here, so
+  expect a second refusal behind the first.
+* Bucket 1 is where the leverage is: **10 of 43** tests, one emitter change, and the same
+  change almost certainly covers roottest and `math/*` elsewhere — the survey
+  (`tools/cint_survey.py --top 20`) should be asked how often `&v[0]`/`&v.front()` appears
+  before the fix is scoped.
